license: MIT
version: 0.4.1
date-released: 2026-05-14
keywords:
  - WebGPU
  - quantum chemistry
  - coupled cluster
  - CCSD(T)
  - EOM-CCSD
  - density functional theory
  - density fitting
  - matrix product states
  - DMRG
  - kernel fusion
  - in-browser computation
  - reproducible research
abstract: >-
  webgpu-q is a research-grade quantum chemistry and many-body physics
  engine that runs entirely in a web browser via the WebGPU compute
  API. Methods include RHF/UHF/DFT/MP2/CCSD/UCCSD/CCSD(T), Cholesky
  density fitting (DF-HF, DF-MP2), and EE/IP/EA-EOM-CCSD for excited
  states / ionization / electron attachment. CCSD(T) on H2O cc-pVDZ
  is accelerated 39.3× via a WGSL compute kernel. The Stanton-Bartlett
  σ-equation implementation of EOM-CCSD is validated against
  brute-force projection of H̄ = e^(-T̂) H e^(T̂) in the full Fock
  space on H2 STO-3G to 10⁻⁵ Ha. Many-body sector covers GPU
  statevector simulation (F ≥ 0.999999 vs CPU), MPS / DMRG with
  Lanczos and MPO (N = 128 TFIM/Heisenberg in browser), and kernel
  fusion (4.18× headline).
